[Music]  the plateau straight on symmetrical way  that wes shoots things feels like a  graphic novel it feels like a  presentation the colors the way he  lights things he has exactly what he  wants in mind and he fools you sometimes  into thinking  that this is so formal the shots are so  formal the the moo camera moves are so  formal it might squash  emotion or it might squash spontaneity  and it has the exact opposite effect  you feel comfortable in his visual style  and you know you're going to be  surprised all the time with whatever  he's doing  when i first read this script  it was clear to me  that every sentence as i read the first  30 pages was an entirely new set  and i  knew right away that that's something  that came from  animation  where every shot is its own set and that  that  kind of attention to microscopic detail  on each shot and each beat of a scene is  something that he was going to bring to  live action for the visual base of the  film the costume and the sets and  everything  it's all from research and how do we  shape the research into our story  because you want to try to give it as  much authenticity as you can  one thing that wes does is he sets up a  library of dvds and blu-rays that we  all access most of them were  films from the nouvelle bog the french  new wave  and a lot of the films i had never seen  before so the first thing i do is try to  immerse myself in the world and then we  had a photographic library as well of  reference photos  from  you know the turn of the century all the  way until the 70s and the way paris  which onwe was  loosely based on and they gave us a good  idea of what wes was going for the  biggest challenge on this movie was  volume actually i mean it because it  wasn't one movie it was you know it's  all these different stories interrelated  and so each one of those stories needed  all of its sets i mean how many sets are  there in this film oh god  a lot i think we're up in the 130  range there's a very long sequence that  we film when we first enter  the police station  that set  is a character  and it's also a film making device it's  almost a piece of the camera in the way  that we use it and in the way that you  know the dolly works through it and so  it's designed uh yes as a backdrop to  the scene and yes as a place but it's  also  designed as a very specific filmmaking  tool for wes anderson  police cooking began with the stakeout  picnic and paddy wagon snack but he's  evolved and codified into something  refined intensely nourishing and if  executed properly marvelously flavorful  the whole idea was to make it a little  bit mysterious at first and we were  shooting black and white and you know  jeffrey wright is kind of  walking through the police station  finally he finds it and our key group  sanjay had set up this amazing dolly  which uh two tracks there's one that  goes in and out and one that goes  sideways and we're switching the dolly  in and out it's a very intricate setup  uh jeffrey wright is is is has a  dialogue throughout this whole thing and  we have to make our moves kind of keyed  by what he's saying obviously  it was a real challenge for us and we  all love doing those kinds of shots when  you can pull it off you really get a  sense of  you know kind of exhilaration  the the bare bones of that set we found  at the felt factory and that became a de  facto like film studio in the center of  town it had three or four warehouses  that where we had a construction shop  model making shop  and a couple of shooting stages where we  could set things up and it really became  an actual film studio totally from  scratch  without even electricity when we walked  in the door adam stockhausen's  set design was so amazing like every  little thing that he got just from my  kitchen  was so  extravagant and detailed and oh like the  the stove and all these kind of ancient  kind of  kitchen tools  were everywhere  that was a wonderful location it was in  france and so there was a wealth of  amazing  french things to buy for these sets so i  found all of the copper cookware and the  ovens and all of the  just anything i could possibly want was  at a flea market and i would go once a  month and bring all of that in with  melano the costume designer color  becomes such a key part of how the story  is told you know and just making sure  that those things balance out together  and come out the way that wes is  expecting is a constant process together  elena i mean what's to say she's  incredible  you you show up and you're wearing your  t-shirt and your sweater and your pants  and it's like  what amazing thing are they going to  turn us into today and melania and wes  communicate almost silently and  invisibly but they so understand what  they want he wants to work with  filmmakers and someone like milena  makes the films that she works on as  much as the director does as much as  anybody who's designing  or photographing or in the film  each character is so distinct so  spot on according to her sensibilities  and west's sensibilities  rolling  freeze  sanjay  the key grip sanjay sami who i believe  started working with west on darjeeling  limited he gave me this new appreciation  for  dollywork working the way west works the  artistic precision is so important and  something that i was very inspired by so  when i watch now one of west's film i  can feel the emotional investment of the  movement of the camera i've sat in on  dinner time conversations between wes  and sanjay when they have been  discussing a shot which is seemingly  impossible and  sanjay  will eat his dinner and go okay leave it  with me i'm gonna figure it out and he  does and that is a massive massively  important collaboration they're  reinventing the wheel all the time  [Music]  this film is very much in love with  creativity it's in love with artistry  and  this is i think west's forte is just the  attention to detail  [Music]  we always say we can't understand how he  manages to do it but this one is really  truly something else  kind of blown away when i saw the movie  like how did he do it  i remember when i first started doing  movies  which sort of can we do this  we have a great group of people and i  don't feel like i even bother to say  that anymore  [Music]

